WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.970
A colleague asked me: if we train a

2
00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:06.250
homework helper on our old reports, could

3
00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:08.370
it ever repeat a child's details?

4
00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:11.030
I said no, of course not.

5
00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:14.140
Then I tried a tiny model in the

6
00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:16.760
lab, and it handed back a private

7
00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:18.860
card number, word for word.

8
00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:23.230
Today: why a model can remember too much,

9
00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:26.110
and how to keep people's data out of it.

10
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:28.900
Models are meant to learn

11
00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.320
patterns, not copy lines.

12
00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:34.990
But a rare line, seen in training, can

13
00:00:34.990 --> 00:00:37.750
be stored almost exactly: a phone

14
00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:40.510
number, an address, a pupil's record.

15
00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:42.750
Ask the right way, and the

16
00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:44.250
model finishes it for you.

17
00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:46.930
There's a quieter leak too.

18
00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:50.050
An attacker checks how familiar a sentence

19
00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:52.670
feels to a model, and guesses whether

20
00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:54.670
someone's data was used to train it.

21
00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:57.380
That's called membership inference.

22
00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:01.160
Both are leaks from the model itself.

23
00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:04.550
Nobody shared a file, yet private data

24
00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:05.850
comes out through the answers.

25
00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:09.020
The strongest defence is simple:

26
00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:11.740
keep personal data out of training

27
00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:12.780
you don't need it for.

28
00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:16.190
If you must use real text, remove the

29
00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:19.010
identifiers first: names, phone

30
00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:21.350
numbers, ID numbers and addresses.

31
00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:26.030
In Python, a few lines replace every digit

32
00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:28.150
with a hash before the text is used.

33
00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:31.920
Run it on every file before training, and

34
00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:34.060
no phone number is left to memorise.

35
00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:35.680
Quick check!

36
00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:39.230
We've replaced every digit in our reports.

37
00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:41.860
Are they safe to train on now?

38
00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:43.640
Not yet.

39
00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:46.530
Digits are one kind of identifier.

40
00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:50.960
Names, addresses and unusual details can

41
00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:54.240
still point to a child, so read the text

42
00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:56.400
for those too, or don't use it.

43
00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:59.950
Researchers also add privacy noise

44
00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:02.210
during training, so that no single

45
00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:03.470
example leaves a mark.

46
00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:06.760
It works, but the model gets less useful.

47
00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:09.880
And to test for leaks, plant a

48
00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:12.700
made-up secret, a canary, and check

49
00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:14.640
whether the model ever repeats it.

50
00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:18.350
In the app, a tiny model learns some

51
00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:20.950
school sentences, one of them private.

52
00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:24.610
Ask it to finish the private line, then

53
00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:27.250
raise the privacy and watch the secret

54
00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:30.370
disappear, and the usefulness drop with

55
00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:30.570
it.

56
00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:32.480
Your turn.

57
00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:35.560
A company offers a free marking

58
00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:38.900
assistant, if the school uploads every

59
00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:41.240
pupil's past essays to train it.

60
00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:43.840
What should the school ask first?

61
00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:47.940
Ask what data it needs, whether it

62
00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:50.340
trains on it, and how to delete it.

63
00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:53.180
Remember: models can memorise

64
00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:55.640
rare details, and the leak can

65
00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:57.120
come from the model itself.

66
00:02:57.890 --> 00:03:00.370
Keep personal data out, redact it

67
00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:02.610
first, and test with a canary.

68
00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:05.660
Try it in class tomorrow: give

69
00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:07.640
groups a made-up register with

70
00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:09.780
names, phone numbers and notes.

71
00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:13.120
Groups black out every identifier

72
00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:16.360
by hand, then swap and look for

73
00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:17.240
what the others missed.
